SEARCH-WINDOW SWEEP -- does the reported lead depend on where we looked?
  25 km / M7.5 / 9.1 g | onset +35.4 ms after command | matched short-time, 10 ms dwell
  cruise window pinned to [t_on-0.95, t_on-0.55] so NO search window below overlaps it

               | RULE B: matched per-sample FPR     | RULE C: cruise maximum            
  search_pre   |  median lead          n   pre-cmd |   muD lead   kin lead   ADVANTAGE
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  0.12         |        +76.9         30       77% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.15         |       +101.4         30       83% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.20         |       +165.4         30       93% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.25         |       +211.4         30       97% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.30         |       +267.9         30      100% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.40         |       +357.4         30      100% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0
  0.50         |       +459.4         30      100% |      +31.4      +25.4        +6.0

  RULE B: lead = 1.008 * search_pre -42.1 ms   (r2 = 0.9984, max resid 7.7 ms)
  RULE C: advantage = 0.0000 * search_pre +6.00 ms   (spread 0.0 ms over a 4.2x range)

READING. A slope of 1 means the estimate IS the analyst's window: rule B has no fixed
  point, and 77-100% of the alarms it credits PRECEDE the command that causes the
  maneuver, so they are physically impossible rather than merely early. The event being
  measured lasts 35.4 ms. Rule C is exactly flat over the same range: under a
  zero-false-alarm threshold, where you look does not change what you find.
  (The 130 ms threshold/search overlap in the SHIPPED settings was tested as the cause
   and rejected: removing it moves rule B only +220.4 -> +213.9 ms.)
